  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 138 AM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Precipitation will continue to slowly wind down through \nthe early morning hours as an upper level low pinwheels away to the \neast. While most of the precipitation is falling as rain or drizzle, \ntemperatures in the higher elevations and portions of the Northeast \nKingdom are in the low to mid 30s; our sensor on Mt Mansfield at \njust short of 4000 ft is reading 28F as of 1 am. Therefore expect \nthese areas are seeing periods of snow, though ground temperatures \nshould be warm enough to preclude any accumulation below 2500 ft. \nCameras on some of the higher elevation roads in the Northeast \nKingdom courtesy of VTrans do appear to show a bit of snow \nfalling with no accumulation noted, though it's difficult to \ndiscern for sure due to darkness and thick fog. These lingering \nshowers will taper to an end by mid to late morning or so, \nallowing for a dry Sunday afternoon. Highs will be in the low \n40s to low 50s, with a bit of a brisk wind at 5 to 15 mph with \nlocal gusts to around 20 mph. \n\nAdditional rounds of showers are expected then through the first \nhalf of this week as we remain under the influence of a large upper \nlevel low gyrating over eastern Canada. Multiple shortwaves rotating \naround the base of this low will pivot overhead, keeping the weather \nunsettled over the next few days. Much like our current system, much \nof the shower activity will fall as rain, especially during the \ndaylight hours, but some snow will be possible across the higher \nterrain and portions of the Adirondacks/Northeast Kingdom, \nespecially overnight. Any accumulation will be minimal below 2500 \nft, but we'll be watching closely to see if Mt Mansfield is able to \nbreak the record for latest 1\" of snowfall (Nov 9). Highs will be in \nthe low/mid 40s to low 50s each day, with lows falling into the \nupper 20s to mid/upper 30s. Winds will remain on the breezy side, \nbut not strong enough to cause any significant impacts.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The large Hudson Bay low will continue to dominate the \nweather across our area, bringing multiple shortwaves into region \nthrough the weekend. Wednesday should see lingering, terrain driven \nmix of rain and snow showers throughout the day. Models on Thursday \nremain torn on the timing and placement of warm front ahead of the \ndeveloping low in the Ohio Valley. The solutions could bring a rain-\nsnow mix, or keep things further south and keep our area relatively \ndry during the day. For Friday and the weekend, models are still \nkeying in on significant closed low swinging down through the Mid-\nAtlantic region and then up the coastline, bringing several shots of \nrain and rain/snow mix over the weekend. The current runs are \nleaning towards a wetter solution for our region which could bring \nthe first significant snow of the season. The details should come \ninto better focus over the next few days.",
